  "why": "Lane light-gear, October 30 (the lead's ruling on the light-gear page: \"plain or light-weave cloth, no quilting or stripes\"): a garment (a robe, shirt, tunic, vest, coat or trousers) is cloth from collar to hem, so its cloth regions paint from their own garment paint, never a quilted, striped, plaid or patched cloth. The garment keeps the lining's colours (colourFrom) and swaps its cloth for one of these when the lining rolled another, drawn from its own random source, so no other roll moves. From Normal up the garment wears only the cloths the library keeps clean at its grade (cleanFrom); Janky keeps the rough ones. A garment is one colour: it takes no fade or per-pale finish. Every other cloth region still rolls the full lining set.",
