  "quiet": {
   "parts": ["light-boot-wrapped-l", "light-boot-wrapped-r", "light-turnshoe-l", "light-turnshoe-r"],
   "value": 0.42,
   "grey": 0.35,
   "why": "Lane meshy-quality, November 17 (the lead's GO on the measured audit, docs/production/meshy-quality-2026-11-17): beside the Dead Caller our boots read light, clean and busy, and the Dead Caller's reads dark and quiet. Measured at the game camera, its edge is painted value, not maps or resolution: scanned normal and roughness maps moved nothing, a darker value closed the Retro gap. These parts keep their rolled colours, scaled to this share of their value and eased this share toward grey. The list is the switch: empty it to roll back."
  },
  "smooth": {
   "from": "normal",
   "keep": 0.15,
   "why": "Adam, November 12: \"i rejected the textures that had noisy value ranges except for janky materials\" and \"i would rather have a bunch of smooth textured items with cooler reflective properties than a bunch of noisy yucky looking dusty materials\". From this grade up a material keeps only this share of its speckled and blotchy light and dark: the pattern's soft noise and blotches, hammer dents, scuffs, grime blotches, heathered mottle and cloth cloud, and the armor look's Material Maker breakup, colour drift and mottled iron. Structure stays (weave, mail rings, quilting, scales, inlay, facets, edges), and so do colour, roughness, metal and shine. Janky keeps its noise. Measured by the value-noise guard (ArmorCoatProof.ValueNoise; docs/production/armor-polish-2026-11-12)."
  }
