 "pieceHarmony": {
  "why": "Adam, October 29 (Armor Kit Editor, \"October 29 - colour over metal, reviewed\"): \"things are looking pretty chaotic on the random suits\"; \"since in game we won't be able to have full suit harmony until the player unlocks the painter stainer, how can we get individual pieces to roll more harmonic and less chaotic?\" He approved three parts of the per-piece proposal: each piece is one plan (\"ok yes\"), neutrals and value as glue (\"This is a great strategy\") and Janky stays a little mixed (\"yeah janky stays nasty\"). Floor colour families are deferred (\"for now we embrace the full chaos of the game\"), so nothing here reads the floor.",
  "rule": "A dropped piece rolls one plan (ArmorKitPaint.Theme, from its own seeded plan stream, knowing which slots its look shows). The second plate repeats the plate at secondPlateRepeatByTier. The first plate or second plate that shows sets the plan: the trim, the second plate when it does not repeat, a metal strap and a bare metal's fade into another metal draw only metals whose family that plate's family goesWith, and never a hued metal but the plate's own. Colour: a piece wants 0, 1 or 2 hued colours (huesPerPieceByTier, at most maxHues); its first hue rolls on the grade's bell curve and its second sits beside the first (secondHue); the first regions that show colour (a dyed surface, or a coated metal, on a slot the look paints) take them in turn, every later region repeats one of them at repeatHueShareByTier and is otherwise a neutral. A hued material (huedMaterials) that shows counts as the first hue. The plate surfaces (valueBand.slots) keep their materials and colours within valueBand.halfWidthByTier of the plate's value; trim, straps and cloth keep their own. At scavengedShareByTier a piece is scavenged: it ignores the goes-with list and the hued-metal limit (Janky stays nasty). Look only: no rule, stat or drop rate reads it, and nothing new is saved.",
